4. PRINT ODD AND EVEN NUMBERS ALTERNATELY USING TWO THREADS
==============================================================================

SIMPLE CODE:

class NumberPrinter {

    private int number = 1;
    private final int limit = 10;

    public synchronized void printOdd() {

        while (number <= limit) {

            while (number % 2 == 0) {
                waitSafely();
            }

            if (number <= limit) {
                System.out.println("Odd  : " + number++);
                notifyAll();
            }
        }
    }

    public synchronized void printEven() {

        while (number <= limit) {

            while (number % 2 != 0) {
                waitSafely();
            }

            if (number <= limit) {
                System.out.println("Even : " + number++);
                notifyAll();
            }
        }
    }

    private void waitSafely() {
        try {
            wait();
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        NumberPrinter printer = new NumberPrinter();

        new Thread(printer::printOdd).start();
        new Thread(printer::printEven).start();
    }
}

OUTPUT:

Odd  : 1
Even : 2
Odd  : 3
Even : 4
...
Even : 10

EXPLANATION:

synchronized
    Allows only one thread to execute the method at a time.

wait()
    Releases the monitor lock and pauses the current thread.

notifyAll()
    Notifies other waiting threads.

while is used instead of if because a thread must recheck the condition after
it wakes up.

